WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.060
Here are some tips for knitting socks.

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.650
I'm knitting a small Christmas stocking,
but the techniques are the same.

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.220
First,

4
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:11.940
when you cast on,
no matter what edge you use ribbing or

5
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.840
not, leave a long tail
to use for seaming your sock.

6
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.140
To reduce the number of yarn ends.

7
00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:24.800
Plan ahead when creating
your toes and heels.

8
00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.300
If your heel is going to be the same color
as the rest of your sock

9
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:36.180
and you are instructed to work the heel
on the opposite side from the carriage,

10
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:39.460
notice that the working yarn is
not going to be in position

11
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.400
to knit the heel.

12
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.440
You could break the yarn and put
these needles in hold position,

13
00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.480
rethread the carriage and work the heel.
But you'd have an extra yarn end.

14
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.560
Consider un-knitting one row the needles
that are going to be held.

15
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.880
Positioning that working
yarn next to the heel.

16
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.080
Then work the heel and when it's complete,

17
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.490
knit across all the needles.

18
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:21.840
This eliminates that extra yarn tail.

19
00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:26.490
If your heel is going to be
knit in a second color,

20
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.960
remove the working yarn
from the carriage and secure it.

21
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.600
Free pass over to the heel side.

22
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.100
Since all the needles

23
00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:41.800
are in working position, I just set
my carriage to slip and did a free pass.

24
00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.800
Put the needles in hold as instructed.

25
00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:02.560
Thread up the heel color yarn
and short-row shape the heel.

26
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.340
Now notice I'm using my thumb to make
sure that those stitches don't pop off.

27
00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:15.020
You can use a weight, but manually
holding them in place works pretty

28
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.120
well.

29
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.540
We'll end up with the carriage
on the left in this instance.

30
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.340
Now here's another trick.

31
00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:31.080
Notice how those stitches
on the left are really sloppy.

32
00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.500
I'm going to tighten them up and secure

33
00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:38.660
them, but pick up the heel of that first
stitch of the row below.

34
00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.980
In my case, this little red stitch,

35
00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.260
I'm going to pick that up
and hang it on the needle.

36
00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.320
This will help secure that loose edge.

37
00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.140
To finish up this contrast color heel,
I'll break my white yarn

38
00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:02.020
and notice that the red yarn -  my working
yarn is over there on the right.

39
00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.020
So I'm going to need to free pass.

40
00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.740
This time I'll put all
the needles in hold.

41
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:12.220
My carriage is still
in hold from the short rows.

42
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.320
Free pass over

43
00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:20.560
and my carriage will be on the side of my
working yarn and I can continue knitting.

44
00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.260
Here is the short row
shaping at a larger gauge.

45
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.520
It'll be easier to see

46
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.820
when we first knit across.

47
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:35.920
It should be really clear
why we wrap when we do short rows.

48
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.100
Picture this:

49
00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:42.220
we're going to pass the carriage and that
working yarn back again to the right.

50
00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.280
Can you see where a hole is going to form?

51
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.220
By taking the working yarn and wrapping

52
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:52.220
the needle next to that last
stitch will prevent a hole.

53
00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.640
This is a manual wrap.

54
00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.500
I'll now stitch to an automatic wrap

55
00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:08.840
by putting the needle next
to the carriage into hold position.

56
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:21.080
You can see the wrap and the strand
isn't pulling and creating a hole.

57
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.380
In addition to staying on track

58
00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:46.780
and wrapping each time,
keeping the stitches forming correctly is

59
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.660
the trickiest part
of knitting heels and toes.

60
00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:52.540
Because the knitting is building

61
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:59.040
up in the middle, the stitches tend
to jump up and not form properly.

62
00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:01.780
Use a weight and your fingers

63
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.280
whatever you need to do
to keep things knitting smoothly.

64
00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:13.380
When it's time to start putting needles
back into working position,

65
00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:17.540
next to the carriage
we'll hold the last stitch

66
00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:21.500
and put a needle opposite
the carriage into work

67
00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.320
and knit across.

68
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.300
Then we'll continue putting needles

69
00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:33.560
back into work until all needles are
in working position, completing the heel.

70
00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.940
Just like before,

71
00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.500
we can see that the stitch on the edge

72
00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.880
where we started knitting
can get really sloppy.

73
00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:02.000
By picking up the heel of the stitch from
the row below, you'll tighten things up.

74
00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.980
I cut the white yarn,
put all of the needles into hold.

75
00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.620
The carriage is still set to hold

76
00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:27.980
and I can free pass back over to the other
side where the working yarn,

77
00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:33.480
the red working yarn is sitting there
waiting to continue knitting my sock.

78
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.600
This along with neat seaming
and you'll be hole free.

